-Every option is optional except `password`. The `name` option is worth calling out: it sets the cookie used to store the session and defaults to `h3`. H3 also reads the session from a request header derived from `name`, which it normalizes to lowercase as `x-${name.toLowerCase()}-session`, so the default name `h3` produces the `x-h3-session` header seen earlier. A mixed-case `name` like `MyApp` still resolves to a lowercase `x-myapp-session` header, while the cookie keeps the original casing. That default is why the earlier examples set a cookie named `h3`.
+Every option is optional except `password`.
+
+#### Rotating passwords
+
+`password` also accepts a list. The first password seals new sessions, and the others are only tried when opening a session sealed before the rotation. Sessions sealed with an older password keep working, and they are resealed with the first password the next time they are written (or slid under `idleTimeout`). There is no mass migration.
+
+```ts
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: [
+    process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_NEW!, // seals new sessions
+    process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD!, // only opens sessions sealed before the rotation
+  ],
+});
+```
+
+A few things to keep in mind:
+
+- The list must not be empty, and each password follows the same rules as a single one (at least 32 characters).
+- Each password tried costs one PBKDF2 derivation, so opening a session sealed with the last of N passwords, or an invalid cookie, costs N derivations. Keep lists short (two or three).
+- Remove the previous password from the list once the transition is over. While it stays in the list, anyone who holds it can still forge sessions.
+
+The `name` option is worth calling out: it sets the cookie used to store the session and defaults to `h3`. H3 also reads the session from a request header derived from `name`, which it normalizes to lowercase as `x-${name.toLowerCase()}-session`, so the default name `h3` produces the `x-h3-session` header seen earlier. A mixed-case `name` like `MyApp` still resolves to a lowercase `x-myapp-session` header, while the cookie keeps the original casing. That default is why the earlier examples set a cookie named `h3`.
 
 > [!NOTE]
 > The session cookie defaults to `secure: true`, `httpOnly: true`, `sameSite: "lax"`, and `path: "/"`. Any of these can be overridden via `cookie`.
